{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for P1f phase-A publication at exact head 63401d7bc9604897632b5924107053952894b4b5, tree 73c9e8a9fc74690a4f1adbb095e2e69499e4bd50, base 1c158d8044cd53ceed5cd191f1f423613c3c571e. This is the independent review of request #1111 under my promise #1112 and builder implementation #1082/#1083. The guarded artifact set contains all 23 exact artifacts, with tsconfig.protocol-gates.json / 3b3eaf19e9653d43a85f89c7ea75dff5f6ca7de8 primary. Two material, bounded corrections are required before publication approval.\n\nP1F-01 — P2 — Verify the two successful gate-command outcomes as mandatory evidence.\n\nSource: tests/protocol-gates/authority.test.ts:34–35; scripts/decision-public-evidence.ts:53; scripts/protocol-gate-readiness-evidence.ts:51. The test obtains actual successful outcomes for both commands, removes their normal audit records, and collapses them into an observation. The public evidence verifier skips records without output; the readiness verifier then accepts any truthy observation. It does not verify the two nested results/commands or bind their required positive evidence to raw command/result files.\n\nActual independent reproduction at this source: I copied the author's final public E, replaced the both-commands observation with {results:[],commands:[]}, emptied its publicInputs, and rehashed the changed case, gate-artifacts manifest and E. The actual public verify:protocol-gate-readiness command exited 0 and declared readiness verified. Original source and other raw inputs were unchanged. The altered E SHA-256 is 38d058e84dd2fbae2dbd3a9fc73e309a26cc2c4b90afd008e7909a402d4ad617. This proves an evidence completeness/conformance failure; it does not claim an unsigned E bypasses independent adoption authority or that any real gate passed.\n\nResolution: retain two ordinary command-output audit records, or give this observation an explicit strict contract covering exactly both command names, exit 0, synthetic-only mode, gates:null, and the corresponding retained policy/export/command/stdout/stderr/result bytes. Missing or altered positive results, wrong command or exit, and missing/rehashed public inputs must fail the public verifier after all surrounding digests are recomputed. Audit the other mandatory observation/refusal forms for the same label-only substitution, using bounded type-specific checks. Preserve all existing case coverage and actual positive execution.\n\nP1F-02 — P2 — Bind published artifacts to the actual immutable Git merge receipt.\n\nSource: scripts/decision-validator.ts:115–120. requirePublished currently accepts any standing implementer statement with body.merge_head equal to S and a JSON merge_successors array containing the selected path, provided each new artifact rests on that one statement and is unassigned. It never verifies that S is the sealed GitSeq merge or that the alleged receipt matches its immutable merge parents, approval and succession. Even an ordinary assert can supply these two fields.\n\nActual independent reproduction: using the pinned verifier and generated actors only, I created a signed ordinary implementer assert containing merge_head and merge_successors, filed new copies of the exact archived P1d artifacts on that assert, then created a genuinely signed D/AD/Q/P chain, an actual native guarded self-initiated approved R, and requester AR. No gs merge was performed. The current verifyDecision(...,{requirePublished:true}) accepted the complete 115-case archived evidence and this claimed published set. The synthetic receipt is git:sha1:fcfd973175710876354fa555d5460299c2096844#git:sha1:3e38e9c850fc3e9f80fb925e7ce7760ba6b1169c. The retained probe result SHA-256 is 74fee6984c4ca2317dec06b5d242117d983752eac3822d9042f52106e97efdce. This isolates the publication predicate. It does not bypass the independent signatures, use real Noseq keys, adopt D1–D9, or create a real operational result.\n\nResolution: validate the signed receipt against the actual immutable S Git object and its GitSeq merge receipt, including the target-pre-head/candidate parents and recorded approval, retirement/successor bindings. Pin the selected artifact succession to that actual receipt and retain the current standing/source-world/unassigned checks. The primary pinned GitSeq implementation is internal/mergeplan/mergeplan.go:1087–1141 ReadReceipt and its following decoder: it reads S's trailers, requires the receipt fields, and checks exactly the target-pre-head and candidate parents. Reuse those semantics rather than accepting a self-described alias. Add a negative for this ordinary signed assertion and for mismatching immutable merge/receipt/successor data, plus the actual native publication positive. Keep the decision review distinct from the earlier publication approval.\n\nBoth reproductions and their original inputs remain under artifacts/protocol-gates/reviewer/14033285-31ff-4822-939a-f28e4d4308f2 in the frozen protocol-gates worktree. Finding 01 is in both-commands-observation/review-result.json and verify.log. Finding 02 is in receipt-probe/review-result.json, the generated signed room/export and receipt-probe.mjs/log. An initial helper import-path failure is also retained; it is not the substantive result.\n\nCoverage: I inspected the full request and all three attachments, all 23 changed source/config/test/document paths, exact Git blobs/SHA-256s and signed artifact bindings, the assigned implementation and scope conditions, and root dispositions #1085/#1086 plus the retained #1087 failure. I reused the previously completed P1e/P1d review where source bytes are unchanged. Repo instructions, GitSeq skill/vocabulary and the requested improve audit-playbook sections were read and applied. Protected README, architecture notes, plans, package-lock and Atseq-owned paths are unchanged. I made no tracked source edits.\n\nThe source review covered strict public CLI parsing and real/synthetic separation; immutable executing-source and installed-dependency checks; external policy/principal/minimum-frontier trust; exclusive lock, persistence/rename/fsync and advance-before-rejection high-water semantics; D/AD/Q/P/R/AR chronology and surviving authority; ordinary C_op scope/operator/source/evidence/command bindings; assigned versus published artifact handling; compact @2 inventory and metadata closure; full prerequisite and future-command contracts; public transcript custody; and documentation fidelity. Apart from the findings above, no further material issue emerged within this phase-A scope.\n\nThe new compact formats are justified by retained actual capacity failures, preserve the old @1 contract and existing byte/count caps, and include the full raw closure. Explicit code-unit inventory ordering and actual English/Turkish process/insertion-order positives are present. An additional independent check of the full E closure under en_US and tr_TR yielded identical digests. The two legacy stub assertion edits are the narrowly authorized message changes; the commands continue to exit 1. Generic cache-free CI remains usable and the dedicated gate typecheck runs after preparation.\n\nIndependent verification: clean npm ci, npm run check, npm run build and the complete probe:protocol-gate-readiness all exited 0. This executed the 119 prior runtime cases, 10 authority groups/71 subchecks, and 10 gate groups/61 subchecks. Own final E is artifacts/protocol-gates/runs/1b0e0e75-9eed-4566-bfca-7d11a78e7d83/evidence.json, SHA-256 cb945377faf2bad58fad58c77cbdf5abfe25faaf2af2b43e187a32d646c3cfe8, 27,972 bytes, with 8,350 raw manifest entries. A separate actual Git archive with no prepared dependency cache passed npm ci/check/build. The unchanged 39 closure CLI outcomes matched exact expected exits, and retained stdout/stderr hashes were checked.\n\nAll 123 independent readiness CLI outcomes matched their exact expected exits (one positive, 122 negative). Every retained stdout/stderr digest was checked; all 71 rehashed inherited-subcheck omissions failed specifically at the exact authority subcase contract. Results are artifacts/protocol-gates/cli-challenges/c8dcf826-3294-4f2e-a5c4-c5085c06ca95/results.json, SHA-256 b41ca5a0244fce6f2c1ba8fd9bee4f6f12a264aa5d126f5b507a26e47c685468. The independent review-account.json under the reviewer directory records exact commands, source and evidence hashes; its SHA-256 is 8895fb783904a3cdd009a2667d431c5c4a106c2294ab9d595bdc50cd12f5a25d. Final source status was clean and all 23 artifact bytes remained exact.\n\nThe complete independent detached evidence branch verified 139 case IDs and an exhaustive 12,672-file/141,193,286-byte closure. Its archive SHA-256 is e5a959dd87de0e84b7e54a374ee99c39b37cabeed2c1b0e0e8183c9c65fb0cda; this was data-only imported source, not authority. The actual full inline @1 export was 3,126,194 bytes and correctly failed the unchanged metadata cap. The compact @2 export was 486 bytes, imported the complete same closure at the fixed real F_pre, and correctly exited 1 with MISSING_GENUINE_ADOPTION. Neither emitted a real successful gate result. These records are detached-evidence/c7ad02cb-80e3-4d7d-ae56-e86c59fe7b09 and final-envelope/3cf71a5b-e17d-4589-9c25-938a214f8e80 under artifacts/protocol-gates. The 11 author attachment-account records/drivers were independently hash/size checked; earlier failed/stopped runs and the 1,271,109-byte readiness metadata refusal remain preserved and are not final E.\n\nAll actual authority operations used the separately built clean d630554a7ba7d99c5f95eb77e1dfa7fd1f41637a verifier, binary SHA-256 c2b0ca9c37993e1a3e3e4df809eebd8a07f3af4a4990f9873ba00781c4c28d35, with recorded Go/module/build provenance. I did not rebuild or mutate the shared native strfry or verifier cache and did not attribute the older dirty installed CLI to that source.\n\nThe builder's close-finalization observation is not an additional blocker on current evidence: a HighWater.close failure rejects the invocation and the public CLI exits 1 with failure output; the preceding result.json alone is not a bearer authorization and successful V requires the entire invocation to exit 0. Publishing a success record only after close would clarify retained outcomes, but no authority bypass was established there.\n\nThis verdict blocks phase-A publication approval pending the two corrections and a fresh exact-head review. It is neither D1–D9 selection nor activation. All full G1–G5 gates remain unpassed. The later real S/E/D/AD/Q/P/R/AR and explicit operational commission remain separate ordinary work, with no future hashes or copied result substituting for per-invocation validation. Existing owner-attested control/history custody, old-secret exposure, highest named retained-prefix authorization including unseen-removal exposure, trusted-host/high-water recovery limitations, joint finite caps, incompatible Marmot stop, and P2–P9/deployment exclusions are preserved. Hosted CI for this candidate was not independently run here. No live-key import, operational adoption, deployment, source merge or push occurred.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:3b3eaf19e9653d43a85f89c7ea75dff5f6ca7de8","binding":"assigned","head":"63401d7bc9604897632b5924107053952894b4b5","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:a6fe5bdb0ad3f41112461c1dd7d59ae08cfb6564\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:ee2acf25aeafd274c0ed60b01204d707e5456eda\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:a6fe5bdb0ad3f41112461c1dd7d59ae08cfb6564","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}